# From Objectives to Actions

## Recap and Transition

* From Catalog to Calculus: What This Lesson Builds

- @Key idea@: Lesson 1.1 catalogued the costs of ignoring the business
  objective and of ignoring dynamics; Lesson 2.1 turned causal claims into
  identified, uncertain effects $\Pr(Y|do(a))$; this lesson turns those
  effects into decisions
- Two halves:
  - _Decision theory_: turning a business objective and an interventional
    effect into an optimal action
  - _Dynamic causal ML_: extending that choice to a world that reacts, over
    many steps, not just one
- @Key idea@: by the end of this lesson, the pipeline
  $data \to model \to policy \to action \to feedback$ from Lesson 1.1 is
  fully instantiated, one arrow at a time

* From KPIs to Causal Objectives
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:220 '* Problem 4: How ML Systems Make Decisions Today'

- @Problem@: a KPI like click-through rate or accuracy is a proxy, not the
  goal itself, and a decision system needs a target it can actually optimize
- @Solution@: replace the proxy metric with a utility function $U(y, a)$
  defined directly over outcomes and actions
- @Example@: instead of maximizing predicted click-through rate, maximize
  expected long-term revenue net of the discount cost
- @Key idea@: turning a KPI into a utility function is what makes the
  objective explicit enough to optimize, audit, and defend

# Decision Theory: Utility and Rational Choice

## Utility Functions and Expected Utility

* Utility Functions: Encoding What Matters
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:252 '* Utility Functions'

- @Definition@: a utility function $U: \mathcal{Y} \to \bbR$ maps each
  outcome $y$ to a real-valued preference score, higher is better
- Utilities are:
  - _Subjective_: different stakeholders can have different $U$
  - _Cardinal_: differences, not just rank, carry meaning, defined up to
    positive affine transformations $U \to aU + b$, $a>0$
- @Example@: a hospital utility scale with full recovery $U=1.0$, recovery
  with complications $U=0.7$, death $U=0.0$
  - A 10% reduction in complications is worth 0.03 in expected utility
- @Key idea@: a utility function turns a vague goal like "good outcomes"
  into a number a decision rule can maximize

* The Expected Utility Principle
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:271 '* Expected Utility Principle'
// From: book_springer/related_books.md:26 '- [B028] Kochenderfer, "Decision Making Under Uncertainty", 2015'

- @Fact@: a rational agent chooses the action maximizing expected utility
  under uncertainty
  $$a^* = \argmax_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \EE_{Y \sim \Pr(Y|a)}[U(Y)]$$
- @Theorem@ (von Neumann-Morgenstern, 1944): if preferences over lotteries
  satisfy completeness, transitivity, continuity, and independence, a
  utility function exists such that the agent acts as if maximizing
  $$\EE[U(L)] = \sum_i p_i U(x_i)$$
- @Key idea@: expected utility is not an arbitrary choice of decision
  rule, it is what any preference consistent with those four axioms reduces
  to

* Two Treatments: A Worked Utility Computation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:319 '* Expected Utility: Two Treatment Example'

- @Problem@: a doctor chooses between two treatments
  - Treatment 1: cures with probability 0.9, no side effect
  - Treatment 2: cures with probability 0.95, mild side effect
- Utility scale: cure with no side effect $=1.0$, cure with side effect
  $=0.8$, no cure $=0.0$
  $$\EE[U|T_1] = 0.9(1.0)+0.1(0.0)=0.90$$
  $$\EE[U|T_2] = 0.95(0.8)+0.05(0.0)=0.76$$
- @Key idea@: $T_1$ wins even though $T_2$ cures more often; a higher
  success rate is not the same as a higher expected utility once side
  effects are priced in

* Risk Preferences and the Shape of Utility
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:442 '* Risk Preferences'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:133 '- [P100] Kahneman & Tversky, "Prospect Theory: An Analysis of Decision under Risk", 1979'

- @Intuition@: the curvature of $U$ encodes attitude toward risk
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Profile", "U Shape", "Effect"),
  rows: (
    ("Risk-neutral", "Linear", "Cares only about the mean"),
    ("Risk-averse", "Concave", "Penalizes variance"),
    ("Risk-seeking", "Convex", "Rewards variance"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
- @Definition@: the _certainty equivalent_ is the sure outcome valued as
  much as a risky prospect; for a risk-averse agent it falls below the
  mean, and the gap is the _risk premium_
- @Example@: $U(y) = \log(y)$ is concave; a 50/50 bet between \$100 and
  \$400 has mean \$250 but a certainty equivalent of \$200, a \$50 risk
  premium
- @Key idea@: two decision-makers with the same beliefs can rationally
  choose different actions if their utility curvature differs

* Cost Matrices and Asymmetric Loss, Formally
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson07.2-Posterior_Based_Decisions.smd:96 '* Loss Function: Motivation'

- @Recall@: symmetric losses like log-loss or mean squared error misprice
  asymmetric business errors
- A utility function fixes this directly, by scoring each outcome-action
  pair on the scale that matters to the business, not on prediction error
- @Example@: a fraud utility that penalizes a missed fraud ($U=-50$) far
  more than a false alarm ($U=-1$) drives a more conservative decision
  boundary than accuracy alone ever would
- @Key idea@: a cost matrix is a utility function restricted to a finite
  set of outcomes and actions; expected utility already contains
  cost-asymmetric decision-making as a special case

* Multi-Objective Trade-offs and the Pareto Frontier
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:475 '* Multi-Criteria Trade-offs'

- @Problem@: real decisions weigh multiple objectives that pull in
  different directions: efficacy, cost, patient burden
- @Solution 1@: _scalarization_ collapses objectives into one utility,
  $U(y) = \sum_k w_k U_k(y_k)$, with weights elicited from stakeholders
- @Solution 2@: the _Pareto frontier_, the set of non-dominated actions,
  presents the trade-off visually without committing to weights
- @Example@: recommending a cancer therapy weighs five-year survival,
  quality of life, and cost; the Pareto frontier shows every therapy no
  other therapy beats on all three
- @Key idea@: scalarization commits to a trade-off up front; a Pareto
  frontier defers that commitment to the decision-maker

# Decision Theory: Networks and Bayesian Choice

## From Graphs to Optimal Policies

* Decision Networks: Chance, Decision, and Utility Nodes
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:526 '* Decision Networks: Motivation'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: a _decision network_ extends a causal DAG with three node
  types
  - _Chance nodes_: random variables, as in a Bayesian network
  - _Decision nodes_: variables set by the decision-maker
  - _Utility nodes_: deterministic functions of their parents, yielding
    utility
- @Example@: whether to turn on a sprinkler
  - Chance: rain, whether the grass gets wet
  - Decision: turn on the sprinkler?
  - Utility: lawn health minus water cost
- @Key idea@: a decision network is a compact, graphical specification of
  a decision problem that can be solved automatically
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph DecisionNet {
    rankdir=LR; nodesep=0.6; ranksep=0.6;
    node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];
    Rain [shape=ellipse, style=filled, fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Rain"];
    Grass [shape=ellipse, style=filled, fillcolor="#A6C8F4", label="Grass Wet?"];
    Sprinkler [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#F4A6A6", label="Turn on Sprinkler?"];
    Util [shape=diamond, style=filled, fillcolor="#B2E2B2", label="Lawn Health - Water Cost"];

    Rain -> Grass; Sprinkler -> Grass; Grass -> Util; Sprinkler -> Util;
}
```
::::
:::

* Solving a Decision Network
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:611 '* Solving an Decision Network'

- @Input@: a decision network with conditional probability tables for
  chance nodes and a utility table
- @Output@: an optimal policy $\pi^*$, a function from available
  information to actions
- @Steps@:
  1. Eliminate chance nodes by summing them out
  2. Eliminate decision nodes by maximizing over their domain
  3. Process nodes in reverse topological order
  4. Record the arg-max at each decision node as the optimal policy
- @Key idea@: the same graphical machinery used for probabilistic
  inference also supports optimal decision-making, once utility and
  decision nodes are added

* Bayesian Decision Rules and Admissibility
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:703 '* Why Bayes Rules Are Admissible'

- @Definition@: the _risk_ of a decision rule $\delta$ at parameter
  $\theta$ is its expected loss
  $$R(\theta,\delta) \defeq \EE_{X \sim P_\theta}[L(\theta,\delta(X))]$$
- @Definition@: a rule is _admissible_ if no other rule has risk at least
  as low everywhere, and strictly lower somewhere
- @Theorem@: a rule minimizing Bayes risk
  $r(\delta) = \int R(\theta,\delta)\pi(\theta)d\theta$ is admissible
  - If a dominating rule existed, it would also have lower Bayes risk,
    contradicting optimality
- @Key idea@: a decision rule built to minimize risk under a prior cannot
  be uniformly improved upon, which is what makes the prior more than a
  subjective add-on

* Prior Elicitation: Where Priors Come From
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:797 '* Prior Elicitation: What and Why'

- @Problem@: a causal decision needs a prior over the effect size before
  data arrives, especially when data is scarce, experiments are unethical,
  or stakeholders disagree
- @Methods@:
  - _Quantile elicitation_: ask experts for the 10th, 50th, and 90th
    percentiles of the effect, then fit a distribution
  - _Historical calibration_: compare past expert predictions to actuals
    and correct for bias
- @Pitfalls@: anchoring on the first number mentioned, overconfidence, and
  framing effects that change the elicited answer for the same underlying
  quantity
- @Key idea@: a documented, sensitivity-checked elicitation process is
  what makes a prior defensible rather than arbitrary

* The Value of Information
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:876 '* Expected Value of Perfect Information'

- @Problem@: before committing to a costly action, is it worth gathering
  more data first?
- @Definition@: the _expected value of perfect information_ is the gap
  between deciding with perfect knowledge of $\theta$ and deciding under
  current uncertainty
  $$EVPI = \EE_\theta\left[\max_a U(a,\theta)\right] - \max_a \EE_\theta[U(a,\theta)]$$
- Any information source costing more than the EVPI is not worth acquiring,
  however accurate
- @Key idea@: the value of information is itself a decision-theoretic
  quantity, computed the same way as the value of any other action

# Decision-Focused Learning

* Interventions Inside the Decision
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:343 '* From Observational to Interventional Expectation'

- @Problem@: plugging the observational $\Pr(Y|a)$ into expected utility
  yields a biased action ranking whenever confounders are present
- @Solution@: causal decision theory replaces it with the interventional
  distribution identified in Lesson 2.1
  $$a^* = \argmax_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \EE_{Y \sim \Pr(Y|do(a))}[U(Y)]$$
- @Example@: comparing drugs on observational recovery rates conflates
  who a doctor chose to treat with what the treatment does; adjusting for
  the backdoor set before computing expected utility fixes the ranking
- @Key idea@: decision theory and causal identification are not separate
  topics; expected utility is only valid once its inputs are interventional

* End-to-End, Decision-Focused Learning
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:48 '* Decision Pipeline: What Changes'

- @Problem@: a pipeline that first fits a causal model for accuracy, then
  bolts on a decision rule, optimizes the wrong thing at the first stage
- @Solution@: _decision-focused learning_ fits the model to directly
  maximize downstream decision quality, not intermediate prediction
  accuracy
- @Key idea@: the full pipeline now reads
  $$
  data \to \text{causal model} \to \Pr(Y|do(a))
       \to U(y,a) \to a^* = \argmax_a \EE[U(Y)|do(a)]
  $$
  - Everything up to this point in the book fills in one arrow at a time

# Dynamics: When the World Reacts

## Feedback in Time

* The Static-World Assumption, Formally
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.1-Causal_AI_intro.smd:274 '* Problem 6: Distribution Shift'

- @Recall@: Lesson 1.1 named the static-world assumption as a cost; the
  tools built so far still assume the structural equations $f_i$, and the
  graph itself, stay fixed after the decision is made
- @Problem@: a sustained policy is not a single $do(a)$, it is $do(a)$
  repeated over time, and the world can update its own mechanisms in
  response
- @Key idea@: the rest of this lesson replaces a single intervention with
  a sequence of interventions in a world whose structure can shift as those
  interventions unfold

* Feedback Loops and Simultaneity in Time Series
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:137 '* Feedback Loops and Simultaneity'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: in time series, cause and effect often influence each other,
  creating _simultaneity_: $Y_t$ and $T_t$ are jointly determined
  - Ordinary regression coefficients become inconsistent and cannot be read
    as causal effects
- @Example@: monetary policy affects inflation, and the central bank sets
  policy based on inflation, closing the loop each quarter
- @Solution@: model the variables jointly, e.g., with a structural vector
  autoregression, and impose timing assumptions to separate the directions
  of influence
- @Key idea@: a feedback loop is a qualitatively different problem from
  confounding, since the treatment and the outcome now cause each other
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph Feedback {
    rankdir=LR; nodesep=0.5; ranksep=0.6;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, penwidth=1.4];
    Tt [label="Policy T_t", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    Yt [label="Outcome Y_t", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Tt1 [label="Policy T_t+1", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    Yt1 [label="Outcome Y_t+1", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    Tt -> Yt; Yt -> Tt1; Tt1 -> Yt1; Tt -> Tt1; Yt -> Yt1;
}
```
::::
:::

* Granger Causality: Prediction Is Not Causation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:308 '* Granger Causality: Intuition'

- @Definition@: $X$ _Granger-causes_ $Y$ if past values of $X$ improve the
  forecast of $Y$ beyond what $Y$'s own past already provides
- Granger causality is a rung-1 predictive notion, not a rung-2
  interventional one
  - $X \to Y$ and $Y \to X$ can both hold, common in economic feedback
    systems
  - Forcing $X$ manually is not guaranteed to move $Y$ the way the forecast
    relationship suggests
- @Example@: a barometer reading Granger-causes storms in the data, since
  it predicts them, but manually raising the barometer needle causes no
  storm; atmospheric pressure drives both
- @Key idea@: a variable can help predict another without causing it, the
  correlation-vs-causation gap from Lesson 1.1, now in a time-indexed form

* When Temporal Structure Misleads
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:286 '* When Temporal Structure Misleads'

- Time ordering rules out some causal directions, but introduces its own
  failure modes:
  - _Spurious regression_: two series that both trend upward look related
    even when unrelated
  - _Seasonality_: a holiday bump can mask or mimic a policy effect
  - _Wrong lag specification_: today's outcome may reflect yesterday's
    treatment, misattributing the effect if the lag is wrong
  - _Regime changes_: a model fit before a shock, e.g., a pandemic, may not
    hold after it
- @Key idea@: temporal data is informative about causal direction, but
  applying cross-sectional causal tools to it without accounting for trend,
  seasonality, and regime shift creates new biases rather than removing old
  ones

* Measuring Effects Under Non-Stationarity: Interrupted Time Series
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:405 '* Interrupted Time Series: Motivation'

- @Problem@: a policy is implemented at one known date for an entire
  population, so no control group exists
- @Solution@: fit the pre-intervention trajectory, forecast what would
  have happened without the intervention, and compare to what actually
  happened
  $$
  Y_t = \beta_0 + \beta_1 t + \beta_2 D_t + \beta_3 (t - t^*) D_t + \varepsilon_t,
  \quad D_t = I(t \geq t^*)
  $$
  - $\beta_2$ is the immediate jump at the intervention; $\beta_3$ is the
    change in trend after it
- @Limitations@: relies on extrapolating the pre-period trend, and is
  vulnerable to co-occurring events near $t^*$
- @Key idea@: interrupted time series uses the unit as its own control,
  so time-invariant confounders cancel automatically, at the cost of
  trusting an extrapolation

* Synthetic Control: A Counterfactual Without a Control Group
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.2-Causal_Inference_for_Time_Series.smd:665 '* Synthetic Control: Motivation'

- @Problem@: even a single-unit comparison needs a counterfactual, and no
  single other unit may resemble the treated one closely enough
- @Solution@: build a weighted combination of untreated units that
  matches the treated unit's pre-treatment trajectory, then use that
  synthetic combination as the counterfactual
- @Example@: estimating the effect of German reunification on West
  German GDP by weighting other OECD economies to reproduce West Germany's
  pre-1990 growth path
- @Key idea@: synthetic control is only as credible as the pre-treatment
  fit; a poor fit before the intervention means the post-period gap cannot
  be trusted as a causal effect

# Sequential Decisions and Exploration

## Exploring Under Uncertainty

* The Exploration-Exploitation Trade-off
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson09.3-Multi_Armed_Bandits.smd:93 '* The Exploration-Exploitation Tradeoff'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:919 '* Exploration vs Exploitation'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: a sequential decision-maker must both exploit the best
  action found so far and explore uncertain actions to learn whether
  something better exists
  - Pure exploitation can lock in a suboptimal choice found early
  - Pure exploration wastes reward on options already known to be poor
- @Example@: a news site choosing which headline to show is a live
  instance of the trade-off, repeated at every visitor
- @Key idea@: the trade-off is unavoidable whenever a decision-maker acts
  repeatedly and learns from the outcomes of its own past actions
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph ExploreExploit {
    rankdir=TB; nodesep=0.5; ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, penwidth=1.4];
    Q [label="Which arm to pull?", fillcolor="#FFE6E6"];
    Ex [label="Exploitation:\nbest known arm", fillcolor="#A6F4A6"];
    Pl [label="Exploration:\ntry uncertain arm", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    Q -> Ex; Q -> Pl;
}
```
::::
:::

* Upper Confidence Bound: Optimism Under Uncertainty
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson09.3-Multi_Armed_Bandits.smd:528 '* Optimism in the Face of Uncertainty'

- @Idea@: be optimistic about arms you are uncertain of, and let that
  optimism drive exploration automatically
- @Algorithm@: pull the arm maximizing an optimism-adjusted estimate
  $$a_t = \argmax_i \left[\hat\mu_i + \sqrt{\frac{2\log t}{n_i}}\right]$$
  - Arms pulled less often carry a larger confidence bonus
  - The bonus shrinks as an arm is explored, converging to its true mean
- @Key idea@: unlike randomly exploring a fixed fraction of the time,
  upper confidence bound exploration is directed by uncertainty, and
  achieves regret that grows logarithmically rather than linearly in time

* Contextual and Non-Stationary Bandits
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson09.7-Advanced_Bandits.smd:108 '* Contextual Bandits: Formal Definition'

- @Definition@: a _contextual bandit_ chooses an action conditioned on
  side information $x$, learning a policy $\pi(x) \to a$ rather than one
  fixed best arm
  - @Example@: personalizing which article to recommend using a reader's
    prior clicks and time of day
- @Problem@: a _non-stationary bandit_ has reward distributions that
  drift over time, so an arm estimated as best early on may no longer be
  best later
  - Sliding-window and discounted estimates down-weight stale observations
    to track the drift
- @Key idea@: both extensions relax a standard bandit's two strongest
  assumptions, that reward depends only on the arm, and that an arm's
  reward does not change over time

* Causal Bandits: Sharing Information Across Interventions
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson11.1-Decision_Making_with_Causal_Models.smd:936 '* Causal Multi-Armed Bandits'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:769 '* Do-Calculus: Formal Framework'

- @Definition@: in a _causal bandit_, each arm is an intervention
  $do(a)$ on a partly known causal graph, rather than an unstructured
  option
- @Key idea@: pulling one arm can inform the value of other, untried
  arms whenever the graph links them, cutting the number of pulls needed
  to find the best intervention
- @Example@: in an advertising campaign, channel, audience, and creative
  interact through a known structure; observing one combination updates
  beliefs about related combinations, not just the one tried
- This connects the bandit machinery directly to the identification tools
  from Lesson 2.1: the graph that identifies a single effect is the same
  graph that lets exploration generalize across arms

# Causal Reinforcement Learning

## Long-Horizon Decisions

* Utilities Over Time and the Bellman Equation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson12.1-Reinforcement_learning.smd:460 '* Bellman Equation'
// From: book_springer/related_books.md:42 '- [B030/B062] Sutton et al., "Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction", 2018'

- @Definition@: with a discount factor $\gamma \in [0,1)$, the utility of
  a policy $\pi$ from state $s$ sums discounted future reward
  $$U^\pi(s) = \EE\left[\sum_{i=0}^\infty \gamma^i R(S_{i+1},\pi(S_i),S_i)\right]$$
- @Theorem@ (Bellman equation): the utility of a state under the optimal
  policy equals the best immediate action plus the discounted utility of
  what follows
  $$
  U(s) = \max_{a \in A(s)} \sum_{s'} \Pr(s'|a,s)
    \left[R(s',a,s) + \gamma U(s')\right]
  $$
- @Key idea@: a long-horizon decision is not fundamentally different from
  the single-step decision in this lesson, it is the same expected-utility
  maximization applied recursively over time

* Structural Causal Models for Sequential Decisions
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson12.1-Reinforcement_learning.smd:1875 '* Structural Causal Models for MDPs'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:92 '- [P042] Hafner et al., "Mastering Atari, Go, Chess and Shogi by Planning with a Learned World Model", 2023'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: a standard Markov decision process describes transitions as
  $\Pr(s'|a,s)$, hiding whether the relationship is a mechanism or a
  coincidence of the logged data
- @Solution@: an SCM for a sequential decision makes the mechanism
  explicit
  $$S_{t+1} := f_s(A_t, S_t, U_s), \quad R_{t+1} := f_r(S_{t+1}, A_t, S_t, U_r)$$
- @Key idea@: a hidden confounder $U$ that affects both the historical
  action and the reward compounds across time steps, distorting credit
  assignment across the entire trajectory, not just one transition
- A policy learned from confounded logged data can perform worse than
  random once deployed, having learned a correlation that does not survive
  the agent's own intervention
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}
```graphviz
digraph SCM_MDP {
    rankdir=TB; nodesep=0.5; ranksep=0.5;
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, penwidth=1.4];
    U [label="Confounder U", fillcolor="#decbe4"];
    A [label="Action a_t", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    R [label="Reward R_t", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    U -> A [color="#CC0000"]; U -> R [color="#CC0000"]; A -> R;
}
```
::::
:::

* Counterfactual Credit Assignment
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson12.1-Reinforcement_learning.smd:1956 '* Counterfactual Reasoning and Credit Assignment'

- @Problem@: standard credit assignment asks whether an action _preceded_
  a later reward, not whether it _caused_ that reward
- @Definition@: the counterfactual individual treatment effect of an
  action, on the same trajectory, asks what the reward would have been
  under a different action at that exact step
  $$\tau_i(a_t) = R(a_t) - R(a'_t) \quad \text{for the same trajectory } i$$
- @Key idea@: answering this requires the structural mechanisms, not just
  the observed trajectory, since the alternative action was never actually
  taken
- @Example@: a delivery robot that turned left at a junction and later
  succeeded needs to know whether turning right would also have succeeded,
  before crediting the left turn

* Deconfounding Off-Policy Evaluation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson12.1-Reinforcement_learning.smd:1978 '* Deconfounding Offline / Off-Policy Data'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson08.5-Do_calculus.smd:280 '* The Back-Door Adjustment'
// From: book_springer/related_papers.md:89 '- [P036] Bottou et al., "Counterfactual reasoning and learning systems: The example of computational advertising", 2013'

- @Problem@: offline reinforcement learning trains on logged data from a
  policy that likely depended on unobserved factors, exactly the setting
  where confounding is most severe
  - A new policy fit on this data can exploit correlations that are
    artifacts of the logging policy, not the environment
- @Solution@: apply the backdoor adjustment from Lesson 2.1 to the
  sequential setting
  $$\EE[R | do(a), s] = \sum_u \Pr(u|s)\,\EE[R|a,s,u]$$
- @Key idea@: off-policy evaluation is identification, applied one time
  step at a time; every assumption from Lesson 2.1, positivity and
  ignorability, applies again here, now per transition

# Roadmap

* Roadmap: The Decision Pipeline, Complete

- Lesson 1.1 catalogued four costs; Lesson 2.1 supplied identification and
  uncertainty quantification; this lesson supplied decision theory and
  dynamics
- The full pipeline promised at the start of Part I is now instantiated
  end to end
  $$
  data \to \text{causal model} \to \Pr(Y|do(a))
       \to U(y,a) \to a^* \to \text{feedback}
  $$
- @Key idea@: every technique in the rest of the book, Gaussian
  processes, policy learning, causal reinforcement learning, deployment and
  monitoring, refines one arrow in this pipeline; none of them replace it
